INT. LUMBER MILL OFFICE, DEPOSITION - DAY

Wood-paneled walls. Sawdust in the corners. A rattling air conditioner. A long folding table with a court reporter's empty chair.

ATTORNEY PHILOMENA OKAFOR-LANE, 40s, in a good grey suit, flips through a thick folder. Her pen taps the rim of a mug. She is very still.

The door opens.

CORNELIUS HARGROVE, 60s, work boots, flannel shirt under a blazer too tight at the shoulders, steps inside. He eases into the chair opposite.

PHILOMENA
Mr. Hargrove. Thank you for coming.

CORNELIUS
You didn't give me a choice.

PHILOMENA
No. I didn't.

CORNELIUS
Is the reporter coming?

PHILOMENA
She's late. We'll begin.

She turns on a small recorder. Sets it between them.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
State your name.

CORNELIUS
Cornelius Hargrove.

PHILOMENA
Employment?

CORNELIUS
Foreman, Pinecrest Lumber. Thirty-one years.

PHILOMENA
On March ninth, were you on shift?

CORNELIUS
I was.

PHILOMENA
Did you see Mr. Dunmore fall?

CORNELIUS
I saw a lot of things.

PHILOMENA
Mr. Hargrove.

CORNELIUS
Yes. I saw him fall.

PHILOMENA
From the catwalk?

CORNELIUS
From the catwalk.

PHILOMENA
Was the railing intact?

CORNELIUS
No.

PHILOMENA
Had you reported it?

CORNELIUS
Four times.

PHILOMENA
To whom?

CORNELIUS
To the owner. To his brother. To the safety man. To the priest.

PHILOMENA
The priest?

CORNELIUS
I tell him everything.

She smiles, almost. Cornelius does not.

PHILOMENA
And what did the owner say?

CORNELIUS
He said wood bends.

PHILOMENA
Wood bends.

CORNELIUS
But men break.

The air conditioner clatters. Philomena closes the folder. Cornelius stares at the recorder as if it were a gun.

PHILOMENA
Mr. Hargrove, would you be willing to say that in open court?

CORNELIUS
I'd rather not.

PHILOMENA
Why?

CORNELIUS
Because I'd lose my pension.

PHILOMENA
You might lose more by staying silent.

CORNELIUS
You don't know that.

PHILOMENA
I know that Mr. Dunmore's widow sits in the next room.

Cornelius shifts. His chair squeaks. He looks at the door.

CORNELIUS
She's here?

PHILOMENA
She asked to sit in.

CORNELIUS
She shouldn't.

PHILOMENA
She wanted to look at you.

He stands. The chair scrapes. He paces to the window, back, then stops at the table, hands flat on the wood.

CORNELIUS
I can't do this.

PHILOMENA
You already are.

He slams his palm on the table. The recorder jumps. Philomena doesn't flinch, but she catches the mug before it tips.

They stare at each other. Then, slowly, both reach for the recorder at once, and their hands touch on the plastic.

CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Turn it on again.

Philomena presses the button. The red light blinks.

CORNELIUS (CONT'D)
My name is Cornelius Hargrove. I saw him fall.
